  THE PROOF (one line):  |p(v)| <= C0 (1+v)^D, so
     |L(p^m)| <= C0^m int_0^inf (1+v)^{Dm} e^{-v} dv
             =  C0^m e int_1^inf w^{Dm} e^{-w} dw  <=  e C0^m Gamma(Dm+1) = e C0^m (Dm)!.

==============================================================================
PART C -- both Watson-Nevanlinna hypotheses now hold (the reduction is complete)
==============================================================================
  H1 (sector): THM-1610(E) -- rotating the standing contour v = rho e^{i phi} over
      |phi| < pi/2 continues Psi(t) = sum_{m>=1} L(p^m) t^m to a sector of opening
      pi(1+D) in tau = t^{1/D}, which EXCEEDS the Watson threshold pi for all D>=1.
  H2 (Gevrey-1): PART B -- |L(p^m)| <= e C0^m (Dm)!, i.e. Gevrey-1 in the SAME tau.
  => If L(p^m)=0 for all m>=1, Psi's asymptotic series is 0, so by optimal truncation
     |Psi(tau)| <= inf_N e C0^N (N)! |tau|^N ~ exp(-c/|tau|) in a sector of opening
     > pi; Phragmen-Lindelof / Watson uniqueness then forces  Psi == 0.

  Optimal-truncation envelope  min_N C0^N N! r^N  ~ exp(-1/(e C0 r)):
   r=|tau|    min_N N! r^N (C0=1)    exp(-1/(e r))
       0.3              1.620e-01        2.934e-01
      0.15              8.201e-03        8.608e-02
      0.08              3.292e-05        1.007e-02
  Sub-exponential decay e^{-c/|tau|} in an opening > pi  =>  Psi == 0. RIGOROUS.

==============================================================================
PART D -- the residual: Psi == 0 (the resolvent) => p == 0
==============================================================================
  Psi(t) = int_0^inf [ 1/(1 - t p(v)) - 1 ] e^{-v} dv.  Psi == 0 means the pushforward
  mu = p_*(e^{-v}dv) has int (w^m) dmu = 0 for all m>=1 with int dmu = 1.
  This is NOT automatic (mu can be non-compact / indeterminate) -- it is the SAME
  Liouville/monodromy step as DvdK's own proof, and is the one remaining piece.
  BUT it is IMMEDIATE on the sign-definite locus: if p([0,inf)) lies in a half-plane
  {Re(e^{i a} w) > 0}, then Re(e^{i a} p) > 0 on [0,inf), so
     Re( e^{i a} L(p) ) = int Re(e^{i a} p) e^{-v} dv > 0,  hence L(p) != 0.
  So a sign-definite p is NEVER in the nullcone -- m=1 already fails.  Check:
           v+1 (>0 on [0,inf)): sign-definite=True, L(p)=2  (nonzero, as forced)
                    v^2+1 (>0): sign-definite=True, L(p)=3  (nonzero, as forced)
      v^2-3v+1 (sign-changing): sign-definite=False, L(p)=0  

SUMMARY
  GAUGE: the Watson data (sector pi(1+D), Gevrey-1 in tau=t^{1/D}) is a function of
  the component D alone, invariant under the scaling gauge p->cp; C0 is covariant.
  So the per-component Watson lemma is well-posed on the gauge quotient.
  LEMMA proved: |L(p^m)| <= e C0^m (Dm)! (one line) closes the Gevrey-1 input HYP-8445.
  Together with THM-1610(E)'s sector, the Watson-Nevanlinna REDUCTION of HYP-8350 is
  COMPLETE: L(p^m)=0 forall m  =>  Psi == 0.  The one residual, Psi==0 => p==0, is
  DvdK's Liouville step, and is immediate on the sign-definite locus.
